 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 17,
   "y": 23,
   "plaque": "hafod_elan",
   "title": "The Drowned Valley",
   "text": "THE ELAN VALLEY. Birmingham Corporation built a chain of dams on the Elan and Claerwen, opened in 1904, to bring clean water to a city of factories and cholera. Farms, a school and a mansion were drowned, and families moved out. The water still travels to Birmingham by gravity alone, without a pump. The valley has been quietly complicated about this ever since."
  },
  {
   "x": 22,
   "y": 12,
   "plaque": "hafod_heft",
   "title": "Hefted Flocks",
   "text": "HEFTING. On open mountain, with no walls, hill ewes learn their own patch of ground and teach it to their lambs. The 'heft' goes with the farm, not the farmer, so a flock is sold with the hill. A hefted flock will not wander off and does not need a fence, which is an irony not lost on the old estates."
  },
  {
   "x": 26,
   "y": 6,
   "plaque": "hafod_kite",
   "title": "The Red Kite",
   "text": "THE RED KITE. By the early twentieth century, the red kite in Britain had been reduced to a handful of birds in the hills of mid Wales, guarded by farmers, landowners and the odd egg collector's worst enemy. Protection since then, and re-introduced birds elsewhere, have given back a population of thousands. Wales still looks after the original."
  },
  {
   "x": 13,
   "y": 19,
   "plaque": "hafod_ffridd",
   "title": "Ffridd",
   "text": "FFRIDD. The Welsh word for the zone between the walled fields and the open hill, where bracken, hawthorn, rowan and gorse grow in a mosaic. It's wonderful for birds, reptiles and butterflies. It's also what dies when a farm gives up grazing or sprays the lot, which is why people fight over it."
  }
 ],
